  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier and mostly sunny weather into next week.\n\nThe weather will be pretty benign for the next few days and for\nthe first half of next week. The cold front that passed through\nis now in the Atlantic. It was cool Saturday with highs in the \nupper 40s to the mid-60s but temperatures should recover a \nlittle early next week. By Tuesday, high temperatures may be in \nthe lower 60s to lower 70s which will be a little warmer than \naverage for this time of year. The low temperatures will still \nbe on the colder side, with lows in the lower 30s to lower 40s. \nElevated areas can expect lows to be in the 20s tonight and the \nnext few nights. A decent pressure gradient over the Mid- \nAtlantic will continue as a surface high persists to our west \nand two propagating lows pass by to our north. This will result \nin cold wind chill values in the 20s and 30s region wide for at \nleast the next three nights (including tonight). By the middle \nof next week, 500mb heights become more zonally oriented \nallowing for the recover of temperatures. This \"warming\" period \nis brief as model guidance is pretty confident on a very large \nmid-level trough swooping as far southward as the Gulf Coast by \nFriday. This impressive feature will likely be carrying a \nsurface low which will bring a chance of precipitation to the \narea towards the end of next week. The colder air that will be \ntraveling with it means there is a chance of some upslope \nprecipitation in the western mountains and widespread sub- \nfreezing temperatures for most of the area. Still, this event is\none week away so, for now, all we have is a signal.",
